  "body": {
    "id": "thm:centered-design-clt",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every triangular array in \\(\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{dense}}\\), \\[\\frac{\\widehat\\tau_n-\\tau_n}{\\sigma_n}\\rightsquigarrow\\mathcal N(0,1).\\] The only randomness is \\((\\mathbf A_n,\\mathbf Z_n)\\); the composition-indexed potential outcomes are fixed, and the Lindeberg condition is derived in \\(\\mathrm{Lemma}~\\ref{lem:reveal-increment-bound}\\) rather than assumed.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Y_{i,n}(z,A)",
      "\\mathbf A_n",
      "\\mathbf Z_n",
      "\\tau_n",
      "\\widehat\\tau_n",
      "\\sigma_n^2",
      "\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{dense}}"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:predictable-variance-stability",
      "def:dense-schedule-class",
      "lem:reveal-increment-bound",
      "thm:exact-pame-variance",
      "thm:dense-projection-limit",
      "lem:martingale-array-clt",
      "def:reveal-martingale"
    ],
    "justification": "Centered Gaussian inference survives dense global dependence when the actual reveal martingale has stable predictable variation.",
    "gap": "LiDing2017, Theorem 4, handles complete treatment randomization conditional on fixed experimental units; Ohlsson1989 handles conventional two-stage sampling; neither covers composition-indexed scores generated by a positive-fraction disjoint partition.",
    "consumer": "The result supplies design-based normal inference for PAME when independent-group coupling is asymptotically invalid.",
    "proof_tex": "The terminal sigma-field in Definition~\\ref{def:reveal-martingale} reveals both randomizations, while \\(\\mathcal F_{n,0}\\) is trivial.  Theorem~\\ref{thm:exact-pame-variance} gives \\(\\mathbb E(\\widehat\\tau_n-\\tau_n)=0\\); hence the Doob decomposition telescopes to\n\\[\n \\sum_{r=1}^{N_n+G_n}D_{n,r}=\\widehat\\tau_n-\\tau_n.\n\\]\nMembership in \\(\\mathcal C_{\\mathrm{dense}}\\) includes Assumption~\\ref{ass:predictable-variance-stability}, namely \\(Q_n/\\sigma_n^2\\xrightarrow{p}1\\).  Lemma~\\ref{lem:reveal-increment-bound} proves, from boundedness, stable arm fractions, and scaled-variance nondegeneracy, the conditional Lindeberg condition for \\(D_{n,r}/\\sigma_n\\).  All increments are square integrable because they are bounded.  Lemma~\\ref{lem:martingale-array-clt} therefore applies and yields\n\\[\n \\frac{\\widehat\\tau_n-\\tau_n}{\\sigma_n}\\rightsquigarrow\\mathcal N(0,1).\n\\]\nThe probability law here is solely the design law of \\((\\mathbf A_n,\\mathbf Z_n)\\); the schedule \\(Y_{i,n}(z,A)\\) was held fixed throughout.",
